    <h1>Exercise Name: Seated Leg Extension (Toes Out Variant)</h1>
     <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>

This 3D animation demonstrates the Leg Extension exercise, specifically highlighting how foot angle shifts muscle emphasis on the quadriceps. The video explains that turning your feet outward ("toes out") targets the inner quadriceps muscle (Vastus Medialis / teardrop muscle), shifting engagement relative to standard leg extensions.

 Exercise Name: Seated Leg Extension (Toes Out Variant)
 Target Muscles: Inner Quadriceps (Vastus Medialis)
 Secondary Muscles: Outer and Middle Quadriceps (Vastus Lateralis, Rectus Femoris, Vastus Intermedius)

</div></div>

<pre>
<h1>

How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1> <u><b><h4>1.Machine Setup</h4></b></u>: Adjust the back pad so your knees line up directly with the machine's pivot axis. Position the padded lever so it rests comfortably against your lower shins just above the ankles.
 <u><b><h4>2.Foot Position</h4></b></u>: Sit back against the seat, grab the handles for support, and rotate your feet slightly outward at the ankles (toes pointed outward at a slight angle).
 <u><b><h4>3.Lifting (Concentric)</h4></b></u>: Exhale as you extend your legs upward until they are nearly straight. Squeeze your quad muscles firmly at the top position.
 <u><b><h4>4.Lowering (Eccentric)</h4></b></u>: Inhale as you slowly lower the padded lever back to the starting position in a controlled motion, maintaining constant tension.


<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Targeted VMO Activation:</b> Pointing toes outward places greater emphasis on the Vastus Medialis (teardrop muscle) near the knee joint.
 <b>◉Knee Joint Stability:</b> Strengthening the inner quad muscle helps improve patellar (kneecap) tracking and overall knee stability.
 <b>◉Isolated Quad Training:</b> Isolates the quadriceps completely without taxing the hips, lower back, or glutes.



<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building | 3 – 4 | 10 – 12 | 60 – 90 seconds |
| Endurance / Tone | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Strength | 3 – 4 | 8 – 10 | 90 seconds |

Avoid using excessive momentum or swinging the weight up; focus on a controlled contraction and a brief hold at the top of each rep.

